# Firebase configuration — copy this file to .env and fill in your values
# Get these from: Firebase Console → Project Settings → Your apps → Web app → SDK setup

VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=your_api_key_here
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=your_project_id.firebaseapp.com
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=your_project_id
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=your_project_id.appspot.com
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=your_messaging_sender_id
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=your_app_id

# Google OAuth Web Client ID — used ONLY for the optional "Connect Google
# Calendar" feature (Phases 4/5), via Google Identity Services directly.
# This is completely separate from login, which uses Firebase email/password
# auth (see src/contexts/AuthContext.tsx) and never expires a user's session
# because of Google OAuth token lifetimes. Find/create a Web client at:
# Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials → "OAuth 2.0 Client
# IDs" → Web application. Add your app's origin(s) to "Authorized JavaScript
# origins" (e.g. http://localhost:5173 and your Vercel domain).
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your_oauth_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com

# ── Notifications — Google Calendar-backed, no server/VAPID/service-account needed ──
#
# Deadline reminders, focus-session alerts, and quick reminders are delivered by
# creating Google Calendar events (with reminder overrides) directly from the
# client using the same Google OAuth token as Calendar sync — see
# src/lib/notificationSync.ts. No extra env vars are required beyond
# VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID above.
#
# NOTE: this project previously used a Web Push (VAPID) + Vercel Cron + Firebase
# Admin pipeline for notifications (Phase 5, since removed). The orphaned
# `users/{uid}/pushTokens` documents are still dead and can go. CRON_SECRET is
# LIVE AGAIN since BUILD-5 - see the web push section below.

# ── Web push — LIVE AGAIN since APP-3, and these three are required ──
#
# APP-3 gave the app a service worker (`public/sw.js`), which is what a push
# notification is delivered to — the thing Phase 5 never had, and the reason
# that pipeline was removed rather than fixed. The keys below are the SAME
# Phase 5 keypair; nothing was regenerated, because regenerating one would
# invalidate every subscription already registered against it.
#
# APP-3 shipped one test notification, pressed for by hand from Settings -> App.
# BUILD-5 sends the real reminders: `api/push-fire.ts`, called once a minute by
# a free outside cron service (cron-job.org), because Vercel Hobby's own cron
# runs once a day. It needs a FOURTH variable:
#
#   CRON_SECRET             — SERVER-ONLY. The cron service sends it as
#                             `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>`, which is
#                             also exactly what Vercel's own cron sends, so a
#                             move to Vercel Pro later changes nothing. It also
#                             signs each notification's "5 min" snooze button.
#                             Any long random string:
#                               node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# CRON_SECRET=...
#
#   VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY   — the keypair's public half. `VITE_`-prefixed on
#                             purpose: the browser needs it to subscribe, and a
#                             VAPID public key is meant to be public.
#   VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY       — SERVER-ONLY. Never give it a VITE_ prefix; Vite
#                             would inline it into the bundle as plain text and
#                             anyone could then send notifications as BASE.
#   VAPID_SUBJECT           — a `mailto:` the push service can reach if this
#                             application misbehaves. Required by the spec.
#
# All three must be set in the Vercel Dashboard as well as here — the public one
# because `VITE_` variables are read at BUILD time, so a deploy without it ships
# a bundle that cannot subscribe at all. `api/push-test.ts` reports a missing
# one by name, because the browser's own failure for a mismatched key names
# neither the key nor the problem.
#
# Generate a fresh pair only for a NEW deployment:
#   npx web-push generate-vapid-keys --json
#
# VITE_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=B...
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=...
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com
#
# (FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT is used again by E2 below — keep it.)

# ── Persistent Google Calendar connection (per-user refresh tokens) ──
#
# WHY: a Google *access* token lives about an hour, and privacy-hardened
# browsers (Brave, blocked third-party cookies) refuse the silent iframe that
# would renew it — so a browser-only connection dies daily. The fix is a
# **refresh token**, which only the authorization-code flow can produce and
# which must never reach a browser. Two serverless functions own it:
#
#   api/google-connect.ts  — trades Google's one-time authorization code for an
#                            access token + a refresh token, and stores the
#                            refresh token at `googleTokens/{uid}` in Firestore.
#   api/refresh-token.ts   — reads that stored token and mints a fresh access
#                            token on demand. No popup, any browser, and it
#                            keeps working while the app is closed.
#
# `googleTokens/{uid}` is denied to every client by firestore.rules; only the
# Admin SDK inside those two functions can read it. One document per Firebase
# user, so accounts never share a Google grant — which is why the old
# ALLOWED_UID lock is gone from this path. BUILD-6 took it out of the Gemini
# routes too; nothing reads it any more (see GEMINI_API_KEY below).
#
# These are SERVER-ONLY secrets — set them in the Vercel Dashboard
# (Project → Settings → Environment Variables), NEVER commit them and NEVER
# prefix them with VITE_ (that would expose them to the browser bundle).
#
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID        — a "Web application" OAuth client id (may be the
#                             same client as VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID above)
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET    — that client's secret
#   FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT— service-account JSON (whole file, as a string),
#                             used to verify the caller's Firebase ID token and
#                             to reach the server-only token collection
#
# ONE-TIME SETUP
# 1. **Publish the OAuth consent screen.** Google Cloud Console → APIs &
#    Services → OAuth consent screen → "PUBLISH APP". This is not optional and
#    not cosmetic: while the app sits in *Testing*, Google expires EVERY refresh
#    token after 7 days no matter which flow minted it, and the user is asked to
#    reconnect weekly. Everything else here is wasted effort without this step.
# 2. Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials → your "Web
#    application" OAuth client. Add, under **Authorized redirect URIs**, one
#    entry per origin the app runs on, each ending in /oauth/callback:
#        http://localhost:3000/oauth/callback   (npm run dev:full — vercel dev)
#        https://<your-app>.vercel.app/oauth/callback
#    Google matches these character for character — no trailing slash, and the
#    scheme/port must be exact. (Plain `npm run dev` on :5173 cannot complete
#    the flow anyway: it serves no api/ routes.)
#    Keep the same origins listed under **Authorized JavaScript origins**.
# 3. Copy the Client ID + Client secret into GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID /
#    GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET.
# 4. Firebase Console → Project Settings → Service accounts → "Generate new
#    private key" → paste the ENTIRE downloaded JSON as the value of
#    FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT (Vercel stores it verbatim; the functions
#    normalise the private key's newlines).
# 5. Deploy the Firestore rules (`firebase deploy --only firestore:rules`) so
#    the googleTokens collection is closed to clients.
# 6. Add all of the above in the Vercel Dashboard, then redeploy
#    (`vercel --prod` from liquid-glass/). In the app: Notifications → Connect
#    Google → approve once. Done, permanently.
#
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your_web_client_id.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your_web_client_secret
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT={"type":"service_account","project_id":"...", ... }
#
# GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN (RETIRED) — before per-user tokens existed, one refresh
# token was minted by hand in the OAuth Playground and pasted here, serving the
# single owner account. Nothing reads it any more: a shared token handed to
# whichever uid asks would let any other account on this Firebase project write
# into the owner's calendar. Delete it from Vercel. The upgrade costs exactly one
# click on Connect Google.

# ── AI agent — Gemini ──
#
# GEMINI_API_KEY is a SERVER-ONLY secret. It must NEVER be given a VITE_ prefix:
# Vite inlines every VITE_-prefixed variable into the client bundle as plain
# text, so a VITE_GEMINI_API_KEY would be readable by anyone who opens devtools
# and would let them spend against this key without limit. There is no
# client-side use for it and there never will be.
#
# The browser never sees this key. Two serverless functions stand in front of it:
#   api/chat.ts   — proxies the in-browser assistant's requests.
#   api/models.ts — lists the Gemini models the key can use.
# Both verify the caller's Firebase ID token and then ask server/keyAccess.ts
# (BUILD-6) whether that account may spend this key: the owner, or an account
# he has switched on in /admin. ALLOWED_UID is no longer read.
# (api/gemini-token.ts, which minted Live API voice tokens, was removed in the
# half-built audit, C3: voice went to the device's own speech instead.)
#
# Get a key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey, then add it in the Vercel
# Dashboard (Project → Settings → Environment Variables) or via:
#   vercel env add GEMINI_API_KEY production
#
# GEMINI_API_KEY=your_gemini_api_key

# ── Atlas — the assistant on the laptop (item 8) ──
#
# Where the app reaches Atlas. Atlas is a separate program in ../atlas/, with
# its own repository; it runs on Shreekant's laptop, holds the agent loop, and
# answers on the local models Ollama has there.
#
# THE SCHEME IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT MUST BE https://.
# This app is served over HTTPS, and a browser flatly refuses to let an HTTPS
# page fetch a plain http:// address ("mixed content"). It fails silently in
# the console, and no CORS setting fixes it. So a bare Tailscale IP —
# http://100.x.y.z:8787 — CANNOT work from the deployed app, however reachable
# it is. The address below is issued by Tailscale itself, with a real
# certificate, by `tailscale serve` on the laptop:
#
#   1. Install Tailscale on the laptop and on the tablet; sign into both with
#      the same account.
#   2. In the admin console (login.tailscale.com/admin/dns): enable MagicDNS,
#      then Enable HTTPS. This is the step that issues the certificate.
#   3. On the laptop, with Atlas running: `tailscale serve --bg 8787`
#      It prints the https:// address. That is the value below.
#
# Nothing is exposed to the internet: `tailscale serve` publishes to the
# tailnet only, Atlas itself listens on loopback, and no port is opened. Do NOT
# swap this for ngrok or any public tunnel — ../atlas/DESIGN.md section 9
# rejects that explicitly.
#
# Unset is a valid state: the app then shows no local models and the assistant
# runs in the browser on Gemini, exactly as it did before item 8.
#
# VITE_ATLAS_URL=https://your-laptop.your-tailnet.ts.net

# ── The MCP server: how Atlas reaches your tasks (item 9) ────────────────────
#
# `npm run mcp` starts BASE as an MCP server. Atlas does not usually
# need you to run it — it launches this itself, as a child process, and talks
# to it over a pipe. Point Atlas at it with ATLAS_MCP_COMMAND / ATLAS_MCP_ARGS
# in ../atlas/.env.
#
# These two values are read from `.env` in THIS folder (not Atlas's), because
# they belong to this app's data rather than to Atlas.
#
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT is the same variable the api/ routes use above, and
# on a deployment it is already set in Vercel. It is NOT set on the laptop by
# default, and it has to be for Atlas to reach your tasks:
#
#   1. Firebase console -> Project settings -> Service accounts
#   2. "Generate new private key". A .json file downloads.
#   3. Paste the WHOLE file below as one line, in single quotes.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE YOU PASTE IT. This key is the Admin SDK: it bypasses
# firestore.rules completely and can read and write every account in the
# project, not just yours. It never leaves the machine — Atlas talks to this
# server down a pipe, not over a socket — but it must never be committed, and
# .env is already in .gitignore. If it ever leaks, revoke it in the same
# console screen you made it on.
#
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT='{"type":"service_account","project_id":"…"}'
#
# ATLAS_FIREBASE_UID is whose tasks Atlas acts on. Deliberately explicit rather
# than "the only account": the key above can reach all of them, so the one it
# is allowed to touch is named rather than guessed. Find it in the Firebase
# console under Authentication -> Users, as "User UID".
#
# ATLAS_FIREBASE_UID=
#
# The timezone dates are resolved in, when the caller names none. Optional;
# defaults to Asia/Kolkata.
#
# ATLAS_TIMEZONE=Asia/Kolkata
